<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- The INTERNET permission is required for development. Specifically,
         the Flutter tool needs it to communicate with the running application
         to allow setting breakpoints, to provide hot reload, etc.

         NOTE: the app itself also needs INTERNET at runtime (the X-link
         extractor and Giphy), and that declaration lives in the MAIN manifest.
         Do not assume this one covers it; it does not apply to release.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>

    <!-- Debug-only cleartext, so the extractor service can be tested against a
         copy running on the dev machine before it is deployed anywhere. Run
         uvicorn in services/extractor, map the port with
         "adb reverse tcp:8000 tcp:8000", then launch with the dart-define
         EXTRACTOR_BASE_URL set to http://localhost:8000 (the recipe is spelled
         out in CLAUDE.md; it cannot be written here because an XML comment may
         not contain a double hyphen).

         adb reverse maps the phone's localhost:8000 to the host's, and Android
         blocks cleartext HTTP by default from API 28 on, so without this the
         request fails before it ever leaves the device.

         Scoped to the debug build on purpose. A release app must not be able to
         send anything in the clear, and putting usesCleartextTraffic in the
         main manifest would allow exactly that.
    -->
    <application android:usesCleartextTraffic="true" />
</manifest>
